# How Godot's SSR Reflects a Room {#video subtitle="Screen-space reflections in Godot 4.7, followed through the source and ported pass by pass" engineVersion=4.7.2-stable commit=ed1daf0bf001b61586d9930840f2f1394092c079 date=2026-11-03 fps=30 width=1920 height=1080}

## Where SSR runs {#c1 number=1 target=245 summary="What SSR adds, the problem it solves, the passes of Godot's frame it is made of, and what switches it on." sources="gating, ssr-normal-roughness, process-ssr, copy-last-frame, env-defaults, half-size-setting"}

### SSR puts the room into the floor {#c1-adds kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom {' {ssrEnabled 0.0} {floorRoughness 0.04} {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.45 -0.8}} {cameraYaw 14.0} {cameraPitch 6.0} {cameraDistance 4.6}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {' 14.0 6.0 4.6} {' 22.0 7.0 4.3} {' 30.0 8.0 4.6} {' 20.0 6.0 4.4}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraPitch} {' {' 7.0}} sentence}}}}}This is the lab's study room, and its floor is a sheet of polished metal.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraPitch} {' {' 6.0}} sentence}}}}}A metal floor shows only what it reflects, so with nothing to reflect it is black.

${overText {' {ssrEnabledRange 0.0 1.0}} "Now Godot's screen-space reflections are on"}.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 26.0}} sentence}}}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.04 0.02}} "The sphere, the blue box, and the red and green walls appear in the floor"}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.02 0.35}} "As the floor grows rougher, the reflections blur"}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.35 0.75}} "At a roughness of seven tenths they stop altogether, because Godot's SSR skips surfaces that rough"}.

### The sky cannot see the teapot {#c1-problem kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCompose panelHidden {append baseRoom {append lowCamera {' {skyReflection 1.0} {ssrComposeStep 1.0} {floorRoughness 0.04}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 12.0 8.0} {' 16.0 9.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.0}} sentence}}}}}${overText {' {skyReflectionRange 0.0 1.0}} "Without SSR, the floor of this room can only reflect the sky"}.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 4.8}} sentence}}}}}The sky knows nothing of the room, so the teapot and the other objects are missing from the floor.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 22.0}} sentence}}}}}${overText {' {ssrComposeStepRange 1.0 3.0}} "So the question is how the floor can reflect what is already on screen, without tracing rays through the scene itself"}.

${cue "key" {' {ssrComposeStep 3.0} {skyReflection 0.0} {blend 1.0}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.04 0.02}} "By the end, you will know how a ray from each floor pixel finds what it reflects, with the help of a pyramid of depths"}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.02 0.3}} "You will also know where the reflected colour comes from, and how it is blurred and blended into the floor"}.

### The passes, in Godot's frame {#c1-map kind=2d graph=frame focus="SSR"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "process-ssr"}}} By default, Godot builds the reflections in five passes.

In order, they are a downsample of the depth, a depth pyramid, the trace of the rays, a blur, and a resolve back to full size.

All five run before the opaque pass, which shades the room's solid surfaces.

${cue "key" {' {callout "ssr-blend"}}} That pass uses their result when it shades the floor.

### Ordered by what they read and write {#c1-graph kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelDag baseRoom}}
${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrHiz" "ssrTrace"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 24.0 12.0} {' 20.0 11.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrPrepassFS"}}}}A frame is a list of passes.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrDownsample"}}}}A pass may start only when everything it reads has been written.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrHiz"}}}}For example, the pyramid pass writes the depth pyramid.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrTrace"}}}}The trace reads that pyramid, ${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrHiz" "ssrTrace"}}}}so the trace must run after the pyramid pass.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrDownsample" "ssrHiz"}}}}In the graph, each arrow runs from the pass that writes a resource to a pass that reads it.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrTrace" "ssrFilter"}}}}Following those arrows from pass to pass ${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrDownsample" "ssrHiz" "ssrTrace" "ssrFilter" "ssrResolve"}}}}gives the whole order.

### Last frame closes a loop {#c1-history kind=2d graph=frame focus="HISTORY, SSR"}

One arrow in the frame runs backwards, from a pass late in the frame to the trace.

${cue "key" {' {callout "copy-last-frame"}}} After the transparent pass, the frame's colour is copied into a texture of its own.

${cue "key" {' {callout "last-frame-color"}}} The next frame's trace reads that copy, to find the colours it reflects.

A resource that is kept from one frame to the next like this is called a history.

The trace still runs before the copy, because what it reads was written by the frame before.

### What switches it on {#c1-settings kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelValues {append baseRoom {' {floorRoughness 0.04}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 22.0 14.0} {' 16.0 11.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "env-defaults"}}}${overText {' {ssrEnabledRange 1.0 0.0}} "SSR is off by default"}.

${overText {' {ssrEnabledRange 0.0 1.0}} "Godot's Environment resource turns it on"}.

${overText {' {ssrMaxStepsRange 32.0 64.0}} "By default, the Environment also limits each ray to sixty-four steps"}.

${overText {' {ssrFadeInRange 0.15 0.6}} "Its fade-in, which defaults to fifteen hundredths, weakens a reflection whose ray is short on screen"}.

${over {' {ssrFadeInRange 0.6 0.15}} {overText {' {ssrFadeOutRange 2.0 5.0}} "Its fade-out, which defaults to two, weakens a reflection whose ray is long on screen"}}.

${overText {' {ssrDepthToleranceRange 0.5 0.1}} "Its depth tolerance is half a unit"}, ${overText {' {ssrDepthToleranceRange 0.1 0.5}} "and chapter four uses it to tell a hit from a ray passing behind a surface"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "half-size-setting"} {ssrFadeOut 2.0}}}Separately from the Environment, a project setting runs SSR at half resolution by default.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.2}} sentence}}}}}So SSR traces only a quarter of the screen's pixels.

${cue "key" {' {callout "gating"}}}${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.6}} sentence}}}}}SSR runs only in Forward+, Godot's clustered forward renderer.

${overText {' {ssrEnabledRange 1.0 0.0}} "Godot also skips it when it renders a reflection probe, or when the background is transparent"}.

### Next: what the trace reads {#c1-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom lowCamera}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {' 18.0 10.0 5.6} {' 30.0 14.0 5.0} {' 24.0 11.0 5.3}}}}}}}

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.15 0.1}} "So SSR is a chain of passes that runs before the opaque pass"}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.1 0.05}} "Its colours come from last frame's image"}.

${cue "key" {' {ssrEnabled 1.0} {floorRoughness 0.1} {blend 1.5}}}The chain starts from two images of what is on screen.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.6}} sentence}}}}}The next chapter shows what those two images hold.

### Where to read {#c1-sources kind=2d sources="gating, ssr-normal-roughness, process-ssr, copy-last-frame, env-defaults, half-size-setting"}

These are the lines of Godot's source that this chapter followed.

They are the gate that switches SSR on, the depth pre-pass, the five passes, the copy, and the defaults.

## Depth, normals, and one thread per pixel {#c2 number=2 target=265 summary="The depth pre-pass writes depth and a normal-roughness buffer; the passes run one thread per pixel at half size; a pixel's position and true normal come back from depth." sources="ssr-normal-roughness, nr-write, encode24, best-fit-table, limiter, ssr-size, pass-downsample, downsample-winner, downsample-normal, geom-normal"}

### The pre-pass writes two images {#c2-prepass kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoInputs panelValues baseRoom}}
${cue "key" {' {inset "nr"} {imageGraph "ssrPrepassFS.normalRoughness"} {imageTitle "views.normal_roughness"} imageCornerTopRight}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 22.0 13.0} {' 16.0 11.0}}}}}}}

${overText {' {pickXRange 150.0 250.0}} "Before any ray is traced, the frame needs two images of what is on screen"}.

${overText {' {pickXRange 250.0 350.0}} "One holds how far each pixel is, and the other holds which way its surface faces"}.

${overText {' {pickXRange 350.0 450.0}} "A reflected ray needs both, because it starts on the surface and leaves it in the mirror direction about the normal"}.

${overText {' {pickXRange 450.0 300.0}} "The first image is the depth that the depth pre-pass already writes"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "ssr-normal-roughness"}}}${overText {' {pickYRange 200.0 240.0}} "With SSR on, that pre-pass also writes a second target, called the normal-roughness buffer"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "nr-write"}}}${overText {' {pickXRange 300.0 210.0}} "The buffer's red, green, and blue hold the surface's normal, in view space"}.

${overText {' {pickYRange 240.0 195.0}} "Each component of the normal runs from minus one to one"}.

${overText {' {pickYRange 195.0 150.0}} "Each component is stored in one byte, with minus one as dark and one as full brightness"}.

${cue "key" {' {inset "nr"} {mark 0.0 0.55 1.0 1.0} {imageMarkText "views.floor"}}}${overText {' {pickXRange 210.0 300.0}} "The floor faces up, along y, so its green is high"}.

${cue "key" {' {inset "nr"} {mark 0.0 0.0 0.3 1.0} {imageMarkText "views.red_wall"}}}${over {' {pickXRange 300.0 40.0}} {overText {' {pickYRange 150.0 100.0}} "The red wall faces right, along x, so its red is high"}}.

### Three bytes for a normal, seven bits for roughness {#c2-pack kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoInputs panelCode {append baseRoom {' {listing "prepass"} {highlight {' "encode"}} {pickX 200.0} {pickY 230.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 14.0 12.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "encode24"}}}${overText {' {pickXRange 200.0 380.0}} "Eight bits per channel cannot hold every direction exactly"}.

${overText {' {pickYRange 230.0 175.0}} "Only the direction matters, because the trace rescales the normal to length one when it reads the buffer"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "cube"}}}}${overText {' {pickYRange 175.0 120.0}} "So Godot first scales the normal until it touches a cube, the box from minus one to one on each axis"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "fit"}}}}${overText {' {pickXRange 380.0 250.0}} "Then it shortens the normal to the length that, once rounded to bytes, points closest to the true direction"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "best-fit-table"} {highlight {' "table"}}}}${overText {' {pickYRange 120.0 170.0}} "Godot looks up that best length in a table, by the normal's direction"}.

${overText {' {pickYRange 170.0 220.0}} "At start-up, Godot fills the table by trying a hundred and twenty-six lengths for each direction of a 1024-by-1024 grid"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "nr-write"} {highlight {' "roughness"}}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.15 0.4}} "The alpha byte holds the roughness"}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.4 0.6}} "The roughness is scaled by one hundred twenty-seven over two hundred fifty-five, so it fills only the lower half of the byte"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "dynamic"}}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.6 0.4}} "A moving object stores one minus the scaled roughness, so its alpha lands in the upper half"}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.4 0.25}} "So a value above one half marks a moving object"}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.25 0.15}} "Undoing the one minus gives its roughness back"}.

### One thread per pixel, at half size {#c2-threads kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPyramid panelCode {append baseRoom {' {hizLevel 0.0} {listing "pyramid"} {highlight {' "winner"}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 22.0 12.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "ssr-size"}}}${overText {' {pickXRange 150.0 420.0}} "Every SSR pass is a compute pass, with one thread for each pixel"}.

${overText {' {pickYRange 220.0 180.0}} "The threads run in groups of eight by eight, so sixty-four pixels share a group"}.

${overText {' {pickXRange 420.0 300.0}} "By default, SSR works at half the screen's width and height"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "pass-downsample"}}}So the first pass, the downsample, shrinks the two images to that size.

${cue "key" {' {callout "downsample-winner"} {highlight {' "level0"}}}}${overText {' {hizLevelRange 0.0 0.5}} "From each two-by-two block of pixels, it keeps the nearest depth"}.

${overText {' {hizLevelRange 0.5 1.0}} "Godot stores depth reversed, one at the near plane and zero at the far one, so the nearest surface has the largest value"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "downsample-normal"} {highlight {' "normal"}}}}${overText {' {hizLevelRange 1.0 0.0}} "It also keeps the normal-roughness of the pixel with that nearest depth, so the depth and the normal still belong to one surface"}.

### A pixel's depth gives back its position {#c2-position kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoInputs panelValues baseRoom}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 14.0 12.0} {' 20.0 11.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 4.8}} sentence}}}}}This story follows one floor pixel in front of the teapot, and names that pixel P.

${overText {' {pickXRange 260.0 295.0}} "Where in the room is P"}?

${overText {' {pickYRange 190.0 202.0}} "The trace knows only P's place on screen, and P's depth"}.

${over {' {pickXRange 295.0 330.0}} {overText {' {pickYRange 202.0 215.0}} "The inverse of the projection turns those two into a point in view space"}}.

${cue "key" {' {pickX -1.0} {pickY -1.0}}}${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.6}} sentence}}}}}That point lands on the floor, so the depth alone gives back P's position.

### The true surface, from neighbouring depths {#c2-geonormal kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoInputs panelValues baseRoom}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 10.0 14.0} {' 16.0 11.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "geom-normal"}}}${overText {' {pickXRange 280.0 255.0}} "A normal map can tilt the stored normal away from the real surface"}.

${overText {' {pickXRange 255.0 230.0}} "So the trace also rebuilds the surface's own normal, from the depths around the pixel"}.

${overText {' {pickYRange 205.0 200.0}} "Chapter four uses that normal to start each ray off the real surface"}.

${overText {' {pickXRange 230.0 275.0}} "Along each screen axis, the trace looks at two pixels on each side of the centre"}.

${overText {' {pickXRange 275.0 320.0}} "From each side's two depths, it predicts what the centre's depth would be if the surface continued in a straight line"}.

${overText {' {pickYRange 200.0 197.5}} "It keeps the side whose prediction is closer to the centre's real depth"}.

${overText {' {pickYRange 197.5 195.0}} "So a depth edge on the discarded side cannot bend the normal"}.

${overText {' {pickYRange 195.0 192.5}} "On each axis, the kept side gives a tangent along the surface"}.

${overText {' {pickYRange 192.5 190.0}} "The cross product of the two tangents is the normal"}.

### Next: a pyramid of depths {#c2-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPyramid panelHidden {append baseRoom {' {hizLevel 0.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {' 18.0 10.0 5.6} {' 26.0 13.0 5.2}}}}}}}

${over {' {hizLevelRange 0.0 2.0}} {overText {' {pickXRange 200.0 380.0}} "So each SSR pixel now has a depth, a normal, and a roughness"}}.

${overText {' {hizLevelRange 2.0 1.0}} "With them, a ray could walk the depth image one pixel at a time"}.

${overText {' {hizLevelRange 1.0 0.0}} "The next chapter makes that walk much shorter"}.

### Where to read {#c2-sources kind=2d sources="ssr-normal-roughness, nr-write, encode24, best-fit-table, limiter, ssr-size, pass-downsample, downsample-winner, downsample-normal, geom-normal"}

These are the lines of Godot's source that this chapter followed.

They are the pre-pass, its encoding of the normal, and the downsample.

## The depth pyramid {#c3 number=3 target=190 summary="Each level of the Hi-Z pyramid keeps the nearest depth of four texels below it, so a ray can skip a whole cell it stays in front of." sources="hiz-texture, mip-count, pass-hiz, hiz-max"}

### One pixel per step is many steps {#c3-why kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTrace panelValues {append baseRoom {' {traceStep 0.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 24.0 13.0}}}}}}}

${overText {' {traceStepRange 0.0 2.0}} "Suppose P, the floor pixel in front of the teapot, sent a ray that tested every pixel it crossed on the screen"}.

${overText {' {traceStepRange 2.0 4.0}} "It would take hundreds of steps, one for each pixel"}.

${overText {' {traceStepRange 4.0 6.0}} "Most of those steps would cross empty floor, where the ray cannot hit anything, so they would be wasted"}.


### The depth map as a height field {#c3-field kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPyramid panelHidden {append baseRoom {' {hizLevel 0.0}}}}}

${overText {view "field" {' {fieldShowRange 0.0 0.5}}} "Here is the half-size depth that the downsample keeps"}.

${overText {view "field" {' {fieldShowRange 0.5 1.0}}} "Each texel is one SSR pixel, laid out as the screen shows it"}.

${overText {view "field" {' {fieldMorphRange 0.0 1.0} {fieldTurnRange 0.0 -15.0}}} "Now raise every texel into a column, as tall as its reversed depth"}.

${overText {view "field" {' {fieldTurnRange -15.0 -30.0}}} "Reversed depth is largest for the nearest surfaces, so they stand tallest"}.

${overText {view "field" {' {fieldTurnRange -30.0 -25.0}}} "A reflected ray travels over these columns"}.

${overText {view "field" {' {fieldTurnRange -25.0 -20.0}}} "It hits a surface where it passes below the top of a column"}.

### Each level keeps the nearest of four {#c3-max kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPyramid panelCode {append baseRoom {append {' {hizLevel 0.0} {listing "pyramid"} {highlight {' "reduce"}}} {view "field" {' {fieldShow 1.0} {fieldMorph 1.0} {fieldTurn -30.0} {fieldLevel 0.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {inset "hizGpu"} {imageGraph "ssrHiz.hiz"} {attr "image.level" "0"} imageCornerBottomRight}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "hiz-texture"}}}${overText {append {' {hizLevelRange 0.0 1.0}} {view "field" {' {fieldLevelRange 0.0 1.0}}}} "Godot builds a pyramid of depths, which lets a ray skip whole stretches of the screen at once"}.

${cue "key" {' {inset "hizGpu"} {attr "image.level" "1"}}}${overText {append {' {hizLevelRange 1.0 2.0}} {view "field" {' {fieldLevelRange 1.0 2.0}}}} "Level zero is that half-size depth, and each level above it is half as wide and half as tall"}.

${overText {' {pickXRange 250.0 290.0}} "So a texel on a higher level covers a square cell of the screen"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "hiz-max"} {highlight {' "max"}}}}${over {append {' {hizLevelRange 2.0 3.0}} {view "field" {' {fieldLevelRange 2.0 3.0}}}} {overText {' {pickXRange 290.0 330.0}} "Each texel keeps the largest of the four depths below it"}}.

${cue "key" {' {inset "hizGpu"} {attr "image.level" "3"}}}${overText {' {pickYRange 190.0 230.0}} "With reversed depth, the largest value is the nearest surface"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "store"}}}}${over {append {' {hizLevelRange 3.0 4.0}} {view "field" {' {fieldLevelRange 3.0 4.0}}}} {overText {' {pickXRange 330.0 270.0}} "So a texel promises that nothing in its cell is nearer than the depth it keeps"}}.

### Halve until a side reaches one {#c3-levels kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPyramid panelCode {append baseRoom {append {' {hizLevel 0.0} {listing "pyramid"} {highlight {' "store"}}} {view "field" {' {fieldShow 1.0} {fieldMorph 1.0} {fieldTurn -30.0} {fieldLevel 0.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {inset "hizGpu"} {imageGraph "ssrHiz.hiz"} {attr "image.level" "4"} imageCornerBottomRight}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "mip-count"}}}${overText {append {' {hizLevelRange 0.0 5.0}} {view "field" {' {fieldLevelRange 0.0 5.0}}}} "Godot makes the levels one after another, with one compute dispatch per level, because each level reads the one below it"}.

${cue "key" {' {inset "hizGpu"} {attr "image.level" "7"}}}${overText {append {' {hizLevelRange 5.0 9.0}} {view "field" {' {fieldLevelRange 5.0 9.0}}}} "It stops when one side of a level is a single texel"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "pass-hiz"} {highlight {' "odd"}}}}${over {append {' {hizLevelRange 9.0 5.0}} {view "field" {' {fieldLevelRange 9.0 5.0}}}} {overText {' {pickXRange 200.0 280.0}} "A level with an odd number of rows or columns cannot be halved evenly"}}.

${over {append {' {hizLevelRange 5.0 2.0}} {view "field" {' {fieldLevelRange 5.0 2.0}}}} {overText {' {pickXRange 280.0 360.0}} "So the level above it reads a third row or column for each texel, and no depth is left out"}}.

### A cell can be skipped when the ray stays in front {#c3-skip kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTrace panelValues {append baseRoom {append spoutCamera {append spoutValues {' {traceStep 0.0}}}}}}}

For this example, P moves from the floor to a pixel on the inside of the teapot's spout.

${overText {' {traceStepRange 0.0 17.0}} "This new P sends its ray across the gap between the spout and the teapot's body, and the ray lands on the body"}.

${cue "key" {' {traceStep 0.0}}}${overText {view "profile" {' {profileShowRange 0.0 0.4}}} "Lay out the depths along that ray, as a cross-section"}.

${overText {view "profile" {' {profileShowRange 0.4 0.7}}} "Left to right is the distance along the ray"}.

${overText {view "profile" {' {profileShowRange 0.7 1.0}}} "Up is reversed depth, so nearer surfaces stand taller"}.

The wall behind the gap is far away, so it stands low, far below the ray.

${overText {' {traceStepRange 0.0 0.2}} "Take the first cell the ray crosses, on level zero, where a cell is a single texel"}.

${overText {' {traceStepRange 0.2 0.6}} "The ray leaves the cell before it reaches the cell's nearest depth, the top of its column"}.

So the ray stays above every column in that cell, and nothing there can be hit.

${overText {' {traceStepRange 0.6 0.8}} "The ray skips the whole cell in one step"}.

On a higher level, the same test lets one step skip a cell many texels wide.

### Next: the march {#c3-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPyramid panelHidden {append baseRoom {append {' {hizLevel 3.0}} {view "field" {' {fieldShow 1.0} {fieldMorph 1.0} {fieldTurn -30.0} {fieldLevel 3.0}}}}}}}

${over {append {' {hizLevelRange 3.0 0.0}} {view "field" {' {fieldLevelRange 3.0 0.0}}}} {overText {' {pickXRange 300.0 220.0}} "So the pyramid is a map of the nearest depths, at every scale"}}.

${overText {view "field" {' {fieldMorphRange 1.0 0.0} {fieldTurnRange -30.0 0.0}}} "The next chapter walks P's ray through it, cell by cell"}.

### Where to read {#c3-sources kind=2d sources="hiz-texture, mip-count, pass-hiz, hiz-max"}

These are the lines of Godot's source that this chapter followed.

They are the pyramid's texture, its levels, and the step that keeps the nearest depth.

## Marching the ray {#c4 number=4 target=310 summary="P's ray starts just off the surface, crosses the screen with its depth changing by one per unit of t, climbs a level on a miss and descends on a hit, and ends at level zero." sources="pass-main, trace-sky, trace-rough, bias, bounce, camera-clip, parametric-t, t-max, start-advance, loop, hit-test, tolerance, level-step"}

### The ray starts just off the surface {#c4-start kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTrace panelCode {append baseRoom {' {traceStep 0.0} {listing "trace"} {highlight {' "bias"}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 22.0 12.0} {' 16.0 11.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "trace-sky"}}}${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraPitch} {' {' 14.0}} sentence}}}}}Before any ray walks the pyramid, the trace skips the sky's pixels, because no surface is there to reflect.

${cue "key" {' {callout "trace-rough"}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.15 0.7}} "It also skips surfaces of roughness seven tenths or more, the cut-off where chapter one's reflections stopped"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "bias"} {floorRoughness 0.15} {highlight {' "reflect"}}}}${overText {' {traceStepRange 0.0 1.0}} "For P, the trace reflects the view direction about P's stored normal"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "bias"}}}}${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 4.6}} sentence}}}}}Then it pushes the start a little off the surface, along the true normal from chapter two.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraPitch} {' {' 12.0}} sentence}}}}}That keeps the ray from hitting its own surface at once.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraPitch} {' {' 10.0}} sentence}}}}}The push is larger where a normal map has tilted the stored normal away from the true one.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraPitch} {' {' 12.0}} sentence}}}}}A tilted normal like that can also reflect the ray into the real surface.

${cue "key" {' {callout "bounce"} {highlight {' "reflect"}}}}${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.6}} sentence}}}}}Such a ray is reflected once more, about the true normal, so it leaves the surface.

### The same ray across the screen {#c4-screen kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTrace panelCode {append baseRoom {append {' {traceStep 15.0} {listing "trace"} {highlight {' "screen"}}} {view "field" {' {fieldTurn -30.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "parametric-t"}}}${overText {view "field" {' {fieldShowRange 0.0 0.5}}} "The trace walks the depth image on the screen, so it needs the ray on the screen too"}.

${overText {view "field" {' {fieldShowRange 0.5 1.0}}} "It takes the point one unit along the ray, and projects the start and that point onto the screen"}.

${overText {view "field" {' {fieldMorphRange 0.0 0.25}}} "Along the line between them, a number called t measures how far a point is from the start"}.

${overText {view "field" {' {fieldMorphRange 0.25 0.5}}} "Godot scales the line so that its depth changes by exactly one for each unit of t"}.

${overText {view "field" {' {fieldMorphRange 0.5 1.0}}} "So the t where the ray reaches a given depth is just that depth minus the start's, with no division"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "camera-clip"}}}If the point one unit along the ray lies behind the camera, it cannot be projected, so the trace first pulls it back to the camera's plane.

${cue "key" {' {callout "t-max"}}}${overText {' {pickXRange 280.0 120.0}} "It also finds the t where the ray leaves the screen, because the trace must stop there"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "start-advance"} {highlight {' "start"}} {pickX -1.0} {traceStep 0.0}}}${overText {view "field" {' {fieldShowRange 1.0 0.0}}} "The first step starts in the next cell on level zero, so the ray never hits its own pixel"}.

### A miss climbs a level, a hit descends {#c4-climb kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTrace panelCode {append baseRoom {append spoutCamera {append spoutCode {' {traceStep 0.0} {listing "trace"} {highlight {' "loop"}}}}}}}}

${overText {view "profile" {' {profileShowRange 0.0 1.0}}} "Back on the spout, P's ray is laid out over the depths again, in the cross-section from chapter three"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "loop"} {highlight {' "cell"}}}}${overText {' {traceStepRange 0.0 0.1}} "The trace walks in a loop, and each iteration tests one cell"}.

${overText {' {traceStepRange 0.1 0.2}} "The tested cell is the one that holds the ray, on the current level"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "edge"}}}}${overText {' {traceStepRange 0.2 0.4}} "First it finds the t where the ray leaves that cell"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "depth"}}}}${overText {' {traceStepRange 0.4 0.6}} "Then it finds the t where the ray reaches the cell's nearest depth, the top of its column"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "hit-test"} {highlight {' "hit"}}}}Here the ray leaves first, so it stays above the whole cell, and the cell is a miss.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "advance"}}}}${overText {' {traceStepRange 0.6 0.8}} "The ray jumps to the cell's edge"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "level"}}}}${overText {' {traceStepRange 0.8 1.0}} "Then it climbs a level, to cells twice as wide, so its next step can skip farther"}.

${overText {' {traceStepRange 1.0 4.0}} "The next three cells, on levels one, two, and three, are misses too, so the ray climbs to level four"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "hit"}}}}${overText {' {traceStepRange 4.0 4.6}} "On level four, the ray is already below the top of the cell"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "advance"}}}}${overText {' {traceStepRange 4.6 4.8}} "So that cell may hold a hit, and the ray stays where it is, without jumping"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "level"}}}}${overText {' {traceStepRange 4.8 5.0}} "Then it descends a level, to test smaller cells at the same spot"}.

### At level zero the hit is final {#c4-hit kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTrace panelCode {append baseRoom {append spoutCamera {append spoutCode {append {' {traceStep 5.0} {listing "trace"} {highlight {' "level"}}} {view "profile" {' {profileShow 1.0}}}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "hit"}}}}Above level zero, a hit only says that the cell may hold a surface.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "level"}}}}${overText {' {traceStepRange 5.0 7.0}} "On levels three and two, the cells may hold hits too, so the ray comes down to level one"}.

${overText {' {traceStepRange 7.0 11.0}} "Twice, the ray climbs to level two over an empty cell, and each time a hit sends it back down to level one"}.

${overText {' {traceStepRange 11.0 12.0}} "A hit on level one takes the ray to level zero"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "tolerance"} {highlight {' "tolerance"}}}}${overText {' {ssrDepthToleranceRange 0.5 0.3}} "At level zero only, the trace uses the depth tolerance, half a unit by default"}.

${overText {' {ssrDepthToleranceRange 0.3 0.1}} "A ray more than the tolerance behind the surface in its cell is taken to pass under it, not to hit it"}.

${overText {' {ssrDepthToleranceRange 0.1 0.3}} "Such a ray moves on to the next cell"}.

${overText {' {ssrDepthToleranceRange 0.3 0.5}} "It stays at level zero, so it does not climb and descend over the same spot again"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "loop"}}}}${overText {' {traceStepRange 12.0 14.0}} "Near the body, the ray steps one cell at a time"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "level-step"} {highlight {' "level"}}}}${overText {' {traceStepRange 14.0 15.0}} "A hit at level zero lowers the level to minus one, and a level below zero ends the loop, on the teapot's body"}.

### Three ways out {#c4-exits kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTrace panelValues {append baseRoom {append spoutCamera {append spoutValues {' {traceStep 17.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" {append {view "profile" {' {profileShow 1.0}}} {' {blend 0.3}}}}

${overText {' {ssrMaxStepsRange 64.0 24.0}} "Besides a hit at level zero, the loop also ends when its sixty-four steps run out, one step per iteration"}.

${overText {' {ssrMaxStepsRange 24.0 8.0}} "Then the ray stops at whatever point it has reached"}.

${over {' {ssrMaxStepsRange 8.0 64.0}} {overText {' {pickXRange 368.0 374.0}} "Or the loop ends when the ray leaves the screen, at the t the trace found at the start"}}.

### The slowest lane sets the time {#c4-lanes kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTrace panelValues {append baseRoom {' {traceStep 64.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 20.0 13.0}}}}}}}

${overText {' {pickXRange 200.0 203.5}} "Back on the floor, P's thread runs in a group of eight by eight, as chapter two showed"}.

${overText {' {pickXRange 203.5 207.0}} "The GPU runs a group's threads in waves, batches of threads that execute together"}.

${overText {' {pickYRange 200.0 207.0}} "The threads of one wave run each instruction together, in lockstep"}.

Each ray's loop runs a different number of iterations, so a wave takes as long as its longest trace.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.15 0.75}} "Sky pixels and rough ones skip the trace, so their threads finish at once"}.

${over {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.75 0.15}} {overText {' {pickYRange 207.0 60.0}} "Those threads then wait, because the group is only done when its last ray is"}}.

### Next: was it a reflection? {#c4-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoTrace panelHidden {append baseRoom {' {traceStep 0.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {' 18.0 10.0 5.6} {' 28.0 12.0 5.0}}}}}}}

${overText {' {traceStepRange 0.0 64.0}} "For P on the floor, the same march takes the ray to the teapot"}.

The next chapter asks whether that hit is a reflection, and what colour it is.

### Where to read {#c4-sources kind=2d sources="pass-main, trace-sky, trace-rough, bias, bounce, camera-clip, parametric-t, t-max, start-advance, loop, hit-test, tolerance, level-step"}

These are the lines of Godot's source that this chapter followed.

They are the trace's start, its ray on the screen, and its loop.

## Is it a hit, and what colour {#c5 number=5 target=225 summary="The trace's end gets a validity, not a yes or no; its colour is last frame's at the reprojected point, compressed before the blur." sources="validity, edge-sky, backface, confidence, margin, fades, reprojection, last-frame-size, last-frame-color, compress"}

### Validity instead of a yes or no {#c5-validity kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoHit panelCode {append baseRoom {' {listing "validate"} {highlight {' "store"}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 22.0 12.0} {' 16.0 11.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "validity"}}}${overText {' {pickXRange 260.0 295.0}} "Chapter four's march has stopped at some point, but that end point may not be a real reflection"}.

${overText {' {pickYRange 200.0 210.0}} "So instead of a yes or no, the trace works out how valid the hit is, from zero to one, and a doubtful hit can still count a little"}.

${over {' {pickXRange 295.0 330.0}} {overText {' {pickYRange 210.0 220.0}} "It stores this validity in the alpha channel of the SSR colour it writes"}}.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.2}} sentence}}}}}Later passes weight the reflection by the validity, so a doubtful hit shows only faintly.

${cue "key" {' {callout "edge-sky"} {highlight {' "edge"}}}}${over {' {pickXRange 330.0 60.0}} {overText {' {pickYRange 220.0 300.0}} "A ray that left the screen, or ended on a sky pixel, gets zero, because no surface on screen is there to reflect"}}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "backface"} {highlight {' "back"}} {pickX -1.0} {pickY -1.0}}}${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 4.8}} sentence}}}}}Godot rejects a hit on a surface that faces away from the ray, but only within two SSR pixels of the start.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.6}} sentence}}}}}That limit is Godot's choice, and farther away the trace never checks which way the surface faces.

### How far the ray ended from the surface {#c5-confidence kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoHit panelCode {append baseRoom {' {listing "validate"} {highlight {' "confidence"}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 14.0 12.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "confidence"}}}${overText {' {ssrDepthToleranceRange 0.5 0.2}} "Next, the trace measures the distance in view space between the ray's end and the surface that the depth image shows at that pixel"}.

${overText {' {ssrDepthToleranceRange 0.2 0.05}} "A factor called the confidence falls from one to zero as that distance grows from zero to the depth tolerance"}.

${overText {' {ssrDepthToleranceRange 0.05 0.5}} "Squaring the confidence makes it fall faster, and the squared confidence multiplies the validity"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "margin"} {highlight {' "margin"}}}}${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 30.0}} sentence}}}}}A hit whose colour would come from near the edge of last frame's image is faded out, over a margin along that edge.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 24.0}} sentence}}}}}That fade keeps the reflection from ending in a hard line.

### The fades never reach one {#c5-fades kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoHit panelCode {append baseRoom {' {listing "validate"} {highlight {' "fade"}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 22.0 12.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "fades"}}}${overText {' {ssrFadeInRange 0.15 0.6}} "The two fades from chapter one also multiply into the validity, and both depend on how long the ray is on the screen"}.

${overText {' {ssrFadeInRange 0.6 0.15}} "The fade in rises from zero toward one as the ray grows longer on screen"}.

${overText {' {ssrFadeOutRange 2.0 5.0}} "The fade out falls from one to zero as the ray's length on screen nears the size of the whole screen"}.

${overText {' {ssrFadeOutRange 5.0 2.0}} "With the defaults, the two fades multiplied together never reach one"}.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.0}} sentence}}}}}Their product peaks at fifty-eight hundredths, for a ray seven hundredths of the screen long.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.4}} sentence}}}}}So SSR never covers more than fifty-eight percent of the sky's reflection under it, as chapter seven shows.

### The colour is last frame's {#c5-where kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoHit panelCode {append baseRoom {' {listing "validate"} {highlight {' "color"}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {inset "last"} {imageGraph "ssrCopyLastFrame.lastFrame"} {imageTitle "views.last_frame"} imageCornerBottomRight}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 26.0 14.0} {' 20.0 11.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "reprojection"}}}${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 24.0}} sentence}}}}}The hit's colour comes from last frame's image, the copy from chapter one.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 20.0}} sentence}}}}}The camera may have moved since then, so the trace finds where the hit point was a frame ago.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 18.0}} sentence}}}}}It takes the point from this frame's screen into the world, and then into last frame's camera.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraPitch} {' {' 9.0}} sentence}}}}}Following a point into last frame's view like this is called reprojection.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.0}} sentence}}}}}Godot follows only the camera's motion, not the motion of the objects.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.3}} sentence}}}}}So a moving object's reflection lags a frame behind.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.6}} sentence}}}}}And a surface that just came into view reads the wrong colour, the one last frame's image held at that spot.

${cue "key" {' {callout "last-frame-color"}}}${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraPitch} {' {' 6.0}} sentence}}}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.15 0.1}} "Last frame's image was copied after its own reflections were drawn, so it already holds them"}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.1 0.04}} "So reflections of reflections build up, one more bounce each frame"}.

### Compressed before it is blurred {#c5-compress kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoHit panelCode {append baseRoom {' {listing "validate"} {highlight {' "compress"}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 14.0 11.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.2}} sentence}}}}}The trace multiplies the colour it read by the validity.

${cue "key" {' {callout "compress"}}}Before the blur of the next chapter, it replaces each colour with ${formulaSaid f"{\frac{c}{1 + L(c)}}" "the colour over one plus its luminance" {'}}.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.6}} sentence}}}}}That way, one bright texel cannot dominate an average.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.4}} sentence}}}}}After the blur, the resolve undoes the division.

### Next: the blur {#c5-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoHit panelHidden baseRoom}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {' 18.0 10.0 5.6} {' 26.0 12.0 5.1}}}}}}}

${over {' {pickXRange 200.0 380.0}} {overText {' {ssrFadeOutRange 2.0 3.0}} "So each SSR pixel now holds a colour, weighted by its validity"}}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.15 0.35}} "The next chapter blurs that colour by the floor's roughness"}.

### Where to read {#c5-sources kind=2d sources="validity, edge-sky, backface, confidence, margin, fades, reprojection, last-frame-size, last-frame-color, compress"}

These are the lines of Godot's source that this chapter followed.

They are the terms of the validity, the reprojection, and the colour.

## Blurring by roughness {#c6 number=6 target=215 summary="Roughness becomes a cone, the cone a circle, the circle a mip level; each mip is a blur of the one before; a bilateral resolve returns to full size." sources="cone, blur-radius, mip-bias, mip-store, mip-level-texture, pass-filter, filter-weights, filter-weight, filter-sample, pass-resolve, resolve-taps, resolve-weights, resolve-uncompress, resolve-flag"}

### Roughness becomes a cone, the cone a circle {#c6-cone kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoBlur panelCode {append baseRoom {' {floorRoughness 0.02} {listing "blur"} {highlight {' "cone"}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 22.0 12.0} {' 16.0 11.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.0}} sentence}}}}}For P, the floor pixel we follow, a mirror floor reflects just the one colour its ray hit.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.02 0.3}} "A rougher floor scatters its reflection, so P should show a blur of the colours around that hit"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "cone"}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.3 0.6}} "Godot models that spread as a cone around the reflected ray, wider for a rougher floor"}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.6 0.3}} "The cone's half-angle is the roughness times ninety degrees"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "blur-radius"} {highlight {' "base" "circle"}}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.3 0.6}} "Where the cone reaches the hit, the largest circle that fits inside it gives the blur radius"}.

### A blur level instead of a blur per pixel {#c6-chain kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoBlur panelCode {append baseRoom {' {floorRoughness 0.3} {listing "blur"} {highlight {' "mip"}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 16.0 11.0} {' 20.0 12.0}}}}}}}

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.3 0.15}} "SSR does not blur each pixel by its own radius"}.

${cue "key" {' {inset "mips"} {imageGraph "ssrFilter.ssrColor"} {imageTitle "views.ssr_color"} {attr "image.level" "1"} imageCornerBottomRight}}Instead, SSR keeps mip levels, a chain of copies of the reflection, ${cue "key" {' {inset "mips"} {attr "image.level" "3"}}}each half the size of the one before and blurrier than it.

${overText {' {pickXRange 260.0 340.0}} "Each pixel then reads the level whose blur is as wide as its own circle"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "mip-bias"} {inset ""}}}${over {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.15 0.65}} {overText {' {pickXRange 340.0 300.0}} "The level is"}} ${formulaSaid f"{\log_2\left(\frac{r \cdot \max(w, h)}{16} + 1\right)}" "the log, base two, of one plus the radius in pixels over sixteen" {'}}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.65 0.3}} "So the bigger the circle, the blurrier the level it picks, with a sixteen-pixel radius picking level one"}.

${overText {' {pickXRange 300.0 360.0}} "For a ray longer than a quarter of the screen, Godot lowers that level a little"}.

### The level, stored per pixel {#c6-miplevel kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoBlur panelCode {append baseRoom {' {floorRoughness 0.3} {listing "blur"} {highlight {' "store"}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {inset "level"} {imageGraph "ssrTrace.mipLevel"} {imageTitle "views.mip_level"} imageCornerBottomRight}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 14.0 12.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "mip-level-texture"}}}${over {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.3 0.55}} {overText {' {pickXRange 220.0 380.0}} "Each pixel's level is kept in a texture of its own, for the passes that follow"}}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "mip-store"}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.55 0.1}} "The texture holds one byte per pixel"}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.1 0.4}} "The byte stores the level divided by fourteen, so levels from zero to fourteen fill its range"}.

### Each level blurs the one before {#c6-filter kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoBlur panelValues {append baseRoom {' {floorRoughness 0.3}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {inset "mips"} {imageGraph "ssrFilter.ssrColor"} {imageTitle "views.ssr_color"} {attr "image.level" "0"} imageCornerBottomRight}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 22.0 12.0} {' 18.0 11.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "pass-filter"}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.3 0.35}} "The chain starts from the colours the trace wrote, at half size"}, ${cue "key" {' {inset "mips"} {attr "image.level" "1"}}}and each level is made from the one before.

${cue "key" {' {callout "filter-sample"}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.35 0.4}} "Each texel of a new level first averages a two by two block of the level before"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "filter-weights"} {inset "mips"} {attr "image.level" "2"}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.4 0.3}} "Then it blurs seven texels across and seven down, with Gaussian weights"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "filter-weight"} {inset "mips"} {attr "image.level" "3"}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.3 0.5}} "Some texels hold no colour, because their trace found no valid hit"}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.5 0.3}} "Each of them is averaged in as black, which darkens the blur around it"}.

${cue "key" {' {inset "mips"} {attr "image.level" "1"}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.3 0.5}} "On level one, such a texel counts only a fifth as much as a valid one, so it darkens the blur less"}.

${cue "key" {' {inset "mips"} {attr "image.level" "5"}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.5 0.3}} "That share grows with each level, and from level five up such a texel counts fully"}.

### Back to full size, without bleeding across edges {#c6-resolve kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoBlur panelValues {append baseRoom {' {floorRoughness 0.3}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {inset "final"} {imageGraph "ssrResolve.final"} {imageTitle "views.final"} imageCornerBottomRight}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 12.0 12.0} {' 18.0 11.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "pass-resolve"}}}${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 4.8}} sentence}}}}}The blurred reflection is still at half size, but the floor is drawn at full size.

${cue "key" {' {callout "resolve-taps"}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.3 0.45}} "So a pass called the resolve brings it up to full size, using the four half-size texels around each full-size pixel"}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.45 0.3}} "It reads each of those four texels at that texel's own blur level"}.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 4.4}} sentence}}}}}Along the teapot's edge, some of those four lie on the teapot, and some on the floor.

${cue "key" {' {callout "resolve-weights"}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.3 0.5}} "So each texel's weight is the product of"} ${formulaSaid f"{e^{-2048\,\Delta d}\; e^{-32\,(1 - n \cdot n')}\; e^{-16\,\Delta r}}" "three falling exponentials" {'}}, one each for depth, normal, and roughness.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.5 0.35}} "Each factor falls as that value moves further from the pixel's"}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.35 0.3}} "That keeps the floor's reflection off the teapot, and the teapot's edge stays sharp"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "resolve-uncompress"}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.3 0.45}} "Before averaging, the resolve undoes the last chapter's division by one plus luminance"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "resolve-flag"}}}${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.6}} sentence}}}}}SSR can also run at full size, and then there is no resolve pass.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.45 0.3}} "Instead, the opaque pass picks each pixel's blur level and undoes the division itself"}.

### Next: into the image {#c6-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoBlur panelHidden {append baseRoom {' {floorRoughness 0.3}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {' 18.0 10.0 5.6} {' 26.0 12.0 5.0}}}}}}}

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.3 0.15}} "So P now has a blurred colour at full size, together with its validity"}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.15 0.05}} "The next chapter asks how much of that reflection the floor shows"}.

### Where to read {#c6-sources kind=2d sources="cone, blur-radius, mip-bias, mip-store, mip-level-texture, pass-filter, filter-weights, filter-weight, filter-sample, pass-resolve, resolve-taps, resolve-weights, resolve-uncompress, resolve-flag"}

These are the lines of Godot's source that this chapter followed.

## Into the final image {#c7 number=7 target=185 summary="The opaque pass blends SSR over the sky's reflection by its validity, fades it out between roughness 0.6 and 0.7, and scales it by the environment BRDF like any probe." sources="reflection-source, f0, sky-reflection, spec-occlusion, ssr-fade, ssr-blend, env-brdf"}

### What the floor had before SSR {#c7-before kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCompose panelCode {append baseRoom {' {skyReflection 1.0} {ssrComposeStep 0.0} {listing "compose"} {highlight {' "sky"}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 22.0 12.0} {' 16.0 10.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "reflection-source"}}}${overText {' {ssrComposeStepRange 0.0 0.5}} "Before SSR is added, the floor's indirect specular light comes from the sky's reflection"}.

${overText {' {ssrComposeStepRange 0.5 1.0}} "Godot reads the sky along the reflected direction, bent toward the normal by the roughness squared"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "spec-occlusion"} {highlight {' "occlusion"}}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.15 0.4}} "Then specular occlusion, an estimate of how much of that reflection is blocked, dims it"}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.4 0.15}} "Godot estimates the occlusion from the brightness of the ambient light"}.

### SSR covers it by its validity {#c7-blend kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCompose panelCode {append baseRoom {' {skyReflection 1.0} {ssrComposeStep 1.0} {listing "compose"} {highlight {' "ssr"}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 14.0 12.0} {' 20.0 11.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "ssr-blend"}}}${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.1}} sentence}}}}}${overText {' {ssrComposeStepRange 1.0 2.0}} "Then SSR is laid over the sky's reflection in proportion to its validity, the alpha from chapter five"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "blend"}}}}${overText {' {ssrComposeStepRange 2.0 2.5}} "The sky's part is scaled by one minus that alpha"}.

${overText {' {ssrComposeStepRange 2.5 3.0}} "Then the SSR colour is added, already multiplied by its validity"}.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.4}} sentence}}}}}So the more valid SSR's hit is, the more the room replaces the sky.

${cue "key" {' {callout "ssr-fade"} {highlight {' "ssr"}}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.15 0.7}} "Between a roughness of six tenths and seven tenths, the opaque pass fades the SSR result out, with a fade separate from chapter five's"}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.7 0.15}} "So the reflections thin away before seven tenths, where the trace stops, instead of vanishing at once"}.

### Then the environment BRDF, like any probe {#c7-brdf kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCompose panelCode {append baseRoom {' {skyReflection 1.0} {ssrComposeStep 3.0} {listing "compose"} {highlight {' "brdf"}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "env-brdf"}}}${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.0}} sentence}}}}}After the blend, Godot scales the floor's whole indirect specular light by the environment BRDF.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraPitch} {' {' 6.0}} sentence}}}}}The environment BRDF is the share of incoming light that a surface reflects toward the eye, for its roughness and the viewing angle.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraPitch} {' {' 4.0}} sentence}}}}}So SSR's colour counts as light arriving at the floor, the same as a reflection probe's.

${cue "key" {' {callout "f0"}}}${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraPitch} {' {' 8.0}} sentence}}}}}The environment BRDF starts from the surface's reflectance seen head on, called f zero.

${overText {' {floorMetallicRange 1.0 0.0}} "If the floor were not metal, its f zero would be four hundredths by default, and the reflection faint"}.

${overText {' {floorMetallicRange 0.0 1.0}} "On a metal like this floor, f zero is the albedo, the floor's own colour, so the reflection is strong and tinted by it"}.

### What it costs {#c7-cost kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelPerformance {append baseRoom {' {highlight {' "ssrTrace" "ssrResolve"}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 22.0 12.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrTrace"}}}}In the lab's port of Godot's passes, timed on the GPU, the trace ${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrTrace" "ssrResolve"}}}}and the resolve are the costliest of the SSR passes.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrTrace"}}}}By default, the trace runs at a quarter of the screen's pixels, but each of its rays may take many steps.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrResolve"}}}}The resolve runs at every full-size pixel, and reads four half-size texels for each.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.0}} sentence}}}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.15 0.8}} "Sky and rough pixels skip the trace"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrFilter" "ssrResolve"}}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.8 0.15}} "But the blur of chapter six and the resolve still visit every pixel, so their cost does not shrink with what SSR finds"}.

### Next: the frame again {#c7-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCompose panelHidden {append baseRoom {' {skyReflection 1.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {' 18.0 10.0 5.6} {' 28.0 12.0 5.0}}}}}}}

${overText {' {skyReflectionRange 1.0 0.0}} "So in this room, the floor reflects the sky where SSR found nothing"}.

${overText {' {skyReflectionRange 0.0 1.0}} "Where SSR found a hit, the floor reflects the room"}.

The last chapter follows P, the floor pixel in front of the teapot, back through the whole frame.

